WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.732 --> 00:00:05.940
JOHN D.E. GABRIELI: ADHD
stands for attention deficit

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.170
hyperactivity disorder.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.189
It's a very big problem.

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:10.680
The current estimates are
that 11% of children--

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.150
more than one out of 10--

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.550
meet the diagnosis
for ADHD, and there's

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.100
been a remarkable growth
in the number of adults who

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.600
meet the diagnostic criteria.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.150
The literature is that
about half of children

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.730
no longer meet the diagnosis
by the time they're adults.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.580
Often there are
remaining difficulties

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.220
so they don't outgrow it
as if they never had it,

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.410
but often they have
considerable improvements

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.870
that shows up in their
educational and occupational

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.100
success.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:35.670
SUSAN WHITFIELD-GABRIELI:
In this study

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.753
we're interested in
investigating the default mode

00:00:37.753 --> 00:00:40.170
network and patients
who had ADHD.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.270
The default mode
network are regions

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.300
that have been known to
be typically activated

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.050
more during rest-- and
those three regions

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.090
are the medial
prefrontal cortex,

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:51.120
the posterior cingulate,
lateral parietal cortices.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.250
The default mode network
is the first time

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.290
we can have a neural
system that reflects

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.180
self-referential processing.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.680
AARON MATTFELD: What's been
found in typically developing

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.330
people is we have this synchrony
in your default mode network,

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.200
so when these regions go
up, the other regions go up.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.540
In ADHD, that's not the case.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.210
And that's been
identified in adults

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.550
and that's been
identified in children.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.740
What it hasn't
been identified is

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.020
people that have been
diagnosed as children

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.400
and then followed
longitudinally,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.540
and that's what's
unique about our sample.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:21.270
So these subjects came in.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.360
We got structural scans,
diffusion weighted scans,

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.690
and one of the scans we got
was this resting state scan.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.550
And so during that
scan, their job

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.460
was just to lie as still
as possible for six minutes

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.090
and think of nothing
in particular.

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:37.200
So in the remitter
group, the question was,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.990
would their brain look
like that of people

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.900
that persisted with their
ADHD diagnosis into adulthood

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.190
or look like controls?

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.320
And what they looked
like was like controls--

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.492
like people that had never
been diagnosed with ADHD.

00:01:48.492 --> 00:01:50.200
SUSAN WHITFIELD-GABRIELI:
In other words,

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.980
it looks like the
remitted ADHD somehow

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.560
were able to normalize their
resting state functional

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.060
connectivity to
the PCC and attract

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.560
the current clinical status.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.140
AARON MATTFELD: It wasn't
that the people that remitted

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.510
were completely like controls
in every way possible.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.390
We also know, at
rest, that there

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:09.750
are these task related networks,
these task positive networks.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:11.790
And we wanted to
see the relationship

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:13.860
between the task
positive networks

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.780
and the default mode
network in our sample.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.170
So in control, they show
an anti-correlation.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.530
So when the default
mode network goes up,

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.430
the task positive
network goes down.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.220
And so what we
wanted to identify

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.950
was whether or not
there is a similar kind

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:32.850
of anti-correlation in the
remitter and persistent group.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:36.690
And what we found was this was
not observed in the remitters

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:37.815
or in the persistent group.

00:02:37.815 --> 00:02:39.523
SUSAN WHITFIELD-GABRIELI:
In other words,

00:02:39.523 --> 00:02:42.150
the persistent and remitted had
significantly less default mode

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.650
network anti-correlations
in regions

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.950
like the dorsolateral
prefrontal cortex.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:47.492
JOHN D.E. GABRIELI:
And that suggests

00:02:47.492 --> 00:02:49.710
that the developmental
history of having ADHD

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.770
does leave an impact that
we can see in the brain.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:55.440
And so it's a little of both.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.550
It's both some remittance
clinically, but also

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.650
some continuation from
the childhood history.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:05.460
First of all, it communicates
that some significant number

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.260
of individuals do improve,
in terms of clinical status.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.710
The second thing is we
hope that the convergence

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.330
of different forms
of evidence will

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:15.180
help make people more
confident about the diagnosis

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.690
and treatment selection.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.480
So this is a small
step forward, but we're

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.210
hoping that convergent,
objective scientific

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.880
information will start
to help make it easier

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:27.067
to see what the correct
useful pathway to help

00:03:27.067 --> 00:03:27.900
a child or an adult.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

